Core OOP Principles (Pillars of OOP)
------------------------------------------------------------
1. Encapsulation
    • Hiding data using private variables.
    • Accessing/modifying data using getter and setter methods.
    • Example: Employee class with private salary and methods like getSa1ary()


2. Inheritance
    • Allows a class to acquire properties and methods from another class
    (parent-child relationship).
    • Example: car class inheriting from a vehicle class.


3. Polymorphism
    Compile-time Polymorphism (Method Overloading):
    • Multiple methods with the same name but different parameters.

    Run-time Polymorphism (Method Overriding):
    • Subclass provides a specific implementation for a method in the parent class.
    • Use of @Override and super keyword.


4. Abstraction
    • Hiding implementation details and showing only essential features.
    • Use of abstract classes and interfaces .





Key OOP Topics
------------------------------------------

1. Classes and Objects
    • Creating classes and objects.
    • Understanding constructors, attributes, and methods.


2.  Constructors
    • Default constructor.
    • Parameterized constructor.
    • Constructor Overloading.


3. Method Overloading and Overriding
    • Overloading: Same method name with different parameter lists.
    • Overriding: Redefining a parent class method in a subclass.


4. Interfaces vs Abstract Classes
    • Differences between abstract classes and interfaces.
    • When to use one over the other.


5. Access Modifiers
    • public: Accessible everywhere.
    • private: Accessible only within the same class.
    • protected: Accessible within the same package and subclasses.
    • default: Accessible within the same package.


6. Static Members
    • static methods and variables shared by all objects of a class.
    • Example: Math.pow() , static counter


7. Final Keyword
    • final variable: Cannot change the value once assigned.
    • final method: Cannot be overridden in a subclass.
    • final class: Cannot be extended.


8. Super Keyword
    • Access parent class methods, constructors, or variables.


9. This Keyword
    • Refers to the current object instance.
    • Used for differentiating between local and instance variables.


10. Encapsulation with Validation
    • Using private variables with validation in setter methods.





Advanced OOP Topics
--------------------------------------------------

1. Inner Classes
    • Classes defined inside another class (e.g., Static Inner Class, Non-Static
    Inner Class).


 2. Composition (Has-A Relationship)
    • One class contains a reference to another class.
    • Example: car class has an Engine class.


3. Aggregation and Association
    • Aggregation: Weak association between objects (e.g., Library and Books).
    • Association: General relationship between two objects.


4. Object Cloning
    • Shallow Copy vs Deep Copy.
    • Using clone() method and Cloneable interface.


5. Singleton Class
    • A class that allows only one instance to be created.
    • Example: Runtime class in Java.


6. Dynamic Method Dispatch
    • Achieved through runtime polymorphism.
    • Example: Calling overridden methods using a parent class reference.


7. Abstract Classes vs Concrete Classes
    • Understanding where to use abstraction.


8. Interfaces and Multiple Inheritance
    • Using interfaces to achieve multiple inheritance in Java.


9. Enums
    • Enum type to represent a fixed set of constants.
    • Example: Days of the Week, Directions.


10. Anonymous Classes
    • Declaring classes without names, often used for short-lived purposes.
    • Example: Event listeners in Java.



Best Practices in OOP
------------------------
    • Use Encapsulation to secure data and ensure controlled access.
    • Avoid excessive Inheritance; prefer Composition where possible.
    • Use Interfaces for defining contracts that multiple classes can implement.
    • Always write toString() methods for meaningful object representation.
    • Use polymorphism to write flexible and extensible code.